---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "How to Read a Financial Statement";
const description =
  "The three financial statements — balance sheet, profit and loss, cash flow — " +
  "what each one tells you, how they connect, and the lines worth checking first.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Fundamentals / Reading a financial statement">
  <h1>How to read a financial statement</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A listed company publishes three statements every quarter. Each answers a
    different question, and reading any one alone will mislead you. Together they
    are the closest thing to an honest description of a business that you can get
    without working there.
  </p>

  <h2>The three statements, and what each answers</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>balance_sheet</code><span class="meta">a snapshot on one date</span></div>
      <p>What the company owns and owes at a point in time. Assets on one side,
        liabilities and equity on the other, and the two always balance because
        every rupee of asset was funded by either a lender or an owner.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>profit_and_loss</code><span class="meta">a period, usually a quarter or year</span></div>
      <p>Revenue earned and costs incurred over that period, ending in profit.
        Sometimes called the income statement.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>cash_flow_statement</code><span class="meta">the same period</span></div>
      <p>Cash that actually moved, split into operating, investing and financing.
        This is the one most retail investors skip and the one professionals read
        first.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why profit and cash are not the same thing</h2>
  <p>
    This is the single most useful idea in the whole subject. Accounting recognises
    revenue when it is <em>earned</em>, not when the money arrives. Sell goods on
    90-day credit in March and the sale appears in that year's profit even though
    no cash has been received.
  </p>
  <p>
    A company can therefore report rising profit for years while its bank balance
    drains — because the "profit" is sitting in receivables that customers have
    not paid. It is a common pattern in businesses that are growing by extending
    increasingly generous credit.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    <a href="/learn/cash-flow/">Cash flow versus profit</a> covers this in more
    detail. If you only ever internalise one idea from the fundamentals section,
    make it this one.
  </p>

  <h2>The balance sheet</h2>
  <div class="formula">Assets = Liabilities + Shareholders' Equity</div>
  <p>
    Read it as: everything the business controls was paid for either with borrowed
    money or with owners' money. Indian companies present it under Schedule III of
    the Companies Act, so the layout is consistent across filings.
  </p>

  <h3>Lines worth checking first</h3>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>borrowings</code><span class="meta">current and non-current</span></div>
      <p>Total debt. Compare it with equity and with operating profit — the
        absolute number means little on its own. See
        <a href="/learn/debt-leverage/">debt and leverage</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>trade_receivables</code><span class="meta">money owed to the company</span></div>
      <p>Growing much faster than revenue is a warning. It means sales are being
        booked but not collected.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>inventories</code><span class="meta">unsold stock</span></div>
      <p>Rising inventory alongside flat sales suggests goods are not moving.
        Eventually it gets written down.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>reserves_and_surplus</code><span class="meta">accumulated retained profit</span></div>
      <p>Profits kept in the business rather than paid out. A long record of
        growing reserves is a sign of genuine accumulated earnings.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>contingent_liabilities</code><span class="meta">in the notes, not on the face</span></div>
      <p>Guarantees, disputed tax demands, pending litigation. Not counted as
        liabilities but they can become real. Large contingents relative to net
        worth deserve attention.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The profit and loss statement</h2>
  <p>It runs top to bottom from revenue to net profit, and each margin along the way tells you something different:</p>
  <div class="formula">Revenue from operations
− Cost of materials / cost of goods sold
= Gross profit
− Employee benefits, other expenses
= EBITDA
− Depreciation and amortisation
= EBIT (operating profit)
− Finance costs
= Profit before tax
− Tax
= Profit after tax</div>

  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>gross_margin</code><span class="meta">pricing power</span></div>
      <p>How much of each rupee of sales survives the direct cost of producing it.
        Stable or rising gross margin suggests the company can pass on input costs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>operating_margin</code><span class="meta">operational efficiency</span></div>
      <p>EBIT divided by revenue. This is the business itself, before financing
        and tax decisions distort the picture.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>other_income</code><span class="meta">read the notes</span></div>
      <p>Interest, dividends, one-off asset sales. If a large share of profit comes
        from here rather than from operations, the headline profit is not really
        describing the business.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>exceptional_items</code><span class="meta">one-offs</span></div>
      <p>Restructuring charges, impairments, litigation settlements. Strip them out
        before comparing years, and be suspicious when "one-off" items appear every
        single year.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The cash flow statement</h2>
  <p>Three sections, and the relationship between them tells a story on its own:</p>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>cash_from_operations</code><span class="meta">CFO</span></div>
      <p>Cash generated by actually running the business. Over several years this
        should broadly track net profit. Persistent divergence is the single
        clearest accounting red flag available to an outside investor.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>cash_from_investing</code><span class="meta">CFI</span></div>
      <p>Usually negative in a growing company, because it is buying plant and
        equipment. Consistently positive CFI can mean the company is selling assets
        to stay afloat.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>cash_from_financing</code><span class="meta">CFF</span></div>
      <p>Borrowing, repayment, equity issues, dividends. A company funding
        operating losses by repeatedly raising debt shows up here first.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>A ten-minute first pass</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Before any valuation work, five checks over five years of data will tell you
    whether a company is worth a closer look:
  </p>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>1. cfo_vs_pat</code><span class="meta">cumulative</span></div>
      <p>Add up five years of cash from operations and five years of net profit.
        If CFO is far below PAT, the profits are not converting into cash.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>2. receivable_days_trend</code><span class="meta">direction matters more than level</span></div>
      <p>Receivables ÷ revenue × 365. Rising steadily means collection is getting
        slower, and revenue quality is deteriorating.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>3. debt_to_equity</code><span class="meta">and its trend</span></div>
      <p>Rising leverage without rising returns means the extra borrowing is not
        producing anything.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>4. return_on_capital</code><span class="meta">ROCE</span></div>
      <p>Consistently above the company's cost of borrowing, or the business is
        destroying value as it grows. See <a href="/learn/roe-roce/">ROE and ROCE</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>5. promoter_pledging</code><span class="meta">in the shareholding pattern</span></div>
      <p>Shares pledged against loans. High pledging creates forced-selling risk
        entirely unrelated to how the business performs. See
        <a href="/learn/promoter-governance/">promoter holding and governance</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>Where to find the filings</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Listed Indian companies file with both exchanges, and the filings are free.
    Quarterly results and annual reports are on the
    <a href="https://www.nseindia.com/" rel="noopener nofollow">NSE</a> and
    <a href="https://www.bseindia.com/" rel="noopener nofollow">BSE</a> websites
    under the company's page, and on the company's own investor-relations section.
    Read the annual report rather than a summary: the notes to accounts are where
    the interesting material lives, and no aggregator reproduces them in full.
  </p>

  <h2>What this has to do with charts</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Nothing, and that is the point. The <a href="/screener/">screener</a> on this
    site measures price behaviour — trend, momentum, participation. It cannot tell
    you whether the underlying business earns a decent return on capital or
    whether its profits are real. Those are separate questions, answered from
    separate documents, and neither substitutes for the other.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/cash-flow/">cash flow versus profit</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/roe-roce/">ROE and ROCE</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/valuation-ratios/">valuation ratios</a>
  </p>
</Base>
